{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":662,"cacheRead":30837,"cacheWrite":849,"totalTokens":32350,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.00033099999999999997,"cacheRead":0.00030837000000000003,"cacheWrite":0.000106125,"total":0.000745695,"totalFromProvider":true},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":0},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"`double_underline` is now a first-class style attribute, and the new behavior works in parsing, combining, markup, ANSI output, HTML, and SVG.\n\n**Changes**\n\n- **`rich/style.py`**\n  - `Style.__init__` accepts `double_underline`.\n  - Added a `double_underline` property.\n  - `double_underline`, `double-underline`, and `du` are registered in `STYLE_ATTRIBUTES`, so they parse in styles and markup (including `not du`).\n  - `get_html_style()` emits `text-decoration: underline double`.\n- **`rich/console.py`**: `export_svg()` emits `text-decoration: underline double;`.\n- **`tests/test_style.py`**: added `test_double_underline`.\n\n**Design choice:** the codebase already had `underline2`, which uses SGR 21, the same code as a double underline. Rather than add a second bit, `double_underline` is an alias for that bit. This means `Style(double_underline=True).underline2` is also `True`, and the two keywords are interchangeable. It is consistent with ADR-0003, which lists `double_underline` as a sibling of `underline`.\n\n**Verified**\n\n- `[double_underline]Sample[/]` and `[double-underline red]Alert[/]` render with `\\x1b[21m` and `\\x1b[21;31m`.\n- `style1 + style2` and `Style.combine()` preserve the attribute.\n- HTML export (both stylesheet and inline modes) and SVG export contain the double-underline rule.\n\n**Behavior change:** `underline2=True` previously produced no CSS in HTML/SVG exports. It now produces the double-underline rule, since it is the same attribute. In HTML and SVG, `double_underline` takes precedence over `underline` if both are set.\n\n**Test results:** the full suite has 12 failures, the same 12 that fail on the untouched repo (card, markdown, and syntax rendering tests), so there are no regressions. I didn't investigate those 12 failures."}}
{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`double_underline` is now a first-class style attribute, and the new behavior works in parsing, combining, markup, ANSI output, HTML, and SVG.\n\n**Changes**\n\n- **`rich/style.py`**\n  - `Style.__init__` accepts `double_underline`.\n  - Added a `double_underline` property.\n  - `double_underline`, `double-underline`, and `du` are registered in `STYLE_ATTRIBUTES`, so they parse in styles and markup (including `not du`).\n  - `get_html_style()` emits `text-decoration: underline double`.\n- **`rich/console.py`**: `export_svg()` emits `text-decoration: underline double;`.\n- **`tests/test_style.py`**: added `test_double_underline`.\n\n**Design choice:** the codebase already had `underline2`, which uses SGR 21, the same code as a double underline. Rather than add a second bit, `double_underline` is an alias for that bit. This means `Style(double_underline=True).underline2` is also `True`, and the two keywords are interchangeable. It is consistent with ADR-0003, which lists `double_underline` as a sibling of `underline`.\n\n**Verified**\n\n- `[double_underline]Sample[/]` and `[double-underline red]Alert[/]` render with `\\x1b[21m` and `\\x1b[21;31m`.\n- `style1 + style2` and `Style.combine()` preserve the attribute.\n- HTML export (both stylesheet and inline modes) and SVG export contain the double-underline rule.\n\n**Behavior change:** `underline2=True` previously produced no CSS in HTML/SVG exports. It now produces the double-underline rule, since it is the same attribute. In HTML and SVG, `double_underline` takes precedence over `underline` if both are set.\n\n**Test results:** the full suite has 12 failures, the same 12 that fail on the untouched repo (card, markdown, and syntax rendering tests), so there are no regressions. I didn't investigate those 12 failures."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"openrouter","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","usage":{"input":2,"output":662,"cacheRead":30837,"cacheWrite":849,"totalTokens":32350,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.00033099999999999997,"cacheRead":0.00030837000000000003,"cacheWrite":0.000106125,"total":0.000745695,"totalFromProvider":true},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":0},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1794167721560,"providerThinkingLevel":"medium","responseId":"msg_011Cgr3mrXnFGwzcqaok25BP","rawStopReason":"end_turn","durationMs":3343,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}}
{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`double_underline` is now a first-class style attribute, and the new behavior works in parsing, combining, markup, ANSI output, HTML, and SVG.\n\n**Changes**\n\n- **`rich/style.py`**\n  - `Style.__init__` accepts `double_underline`.\n  - Added a `double_underline` property.\n  - `double_underline`, `double-underline`, and `du` are registered in `STYLE_ATTRIBUTES`, so they parse in styles and markup (including `not du`).\n  - `get_html_style()` emits `text-decoration: underline double`.\n- **`rich/console.py`**: `export_svg()` emits `text-decoration: underline double;`.\n- **`tests/test_style.py`**: added `test_double_underline`.\n\n**Design choice:** the codebase already had `underline2`, which uses SGR 21, the same code as a double underline. Rather than add a second bit, `double_underline` is an alias for that bit. This means `Style(double_underline=True).underline2` is also `True`, and the two keywords are interchangeable. It is consistent with ADR-0003, which lists `double_underline` as a sibling of `underline`.\n\n**Verified**\n\n- `[double_underline]Sample[/]` and `[double-underline red]Alert[/]` render with `\\x1b[21m` and `\\x1b[21;31m`.\n- `style1 + style2` and `Style.combine()` preserve the attribute.\n- HTML export (both stylesheet and inline modes) and SVG export contain the double-underline rule.\n\n**Behavior change:** `underline2=True` previously produced no CSS in HTML/SVG exports. It now produces the double-underline rule, since it is the same attribute. In HTML and SVG, `double_underline` takes precedence over `underline` if both are set.\n\n**Test results:** the full suite has 12 failures, the same 12 that fail on the untouched repo (card, markdown, and syntax rendering tests), so there are no regressions. I didn't investigate those 12 failures."}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"openrouter","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-5.5","usage":{"input":2,"output":662